.TH Declare-BashScript 1 "October 2026" "Noble Factor" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
Declare-BashScript \- Bash script argument and option parsing helper
.SH SYNOPSIS
source "$(dirname "$0")/Declare-BashScript" <script_path> <long-options> <short-options> [arguments]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Provides argument parsing, usage, error, note, and success functions for bash scripts. Intended to be sourced at the
top of other scripts. Supports long and short options, usage display, and error handling for robust script
development.
.PP
This script provides the following key functionality:
.IP \(bu 2
Argument and option parsing using getopt
.IP \(bu 2
Automatic usage/help display via man pages
.IP \(bu 2
Environment variable loading from .env files
.IP \(bu 2
Standardized error, note, and success message functions
.IP \(bu 2
Error and signal traps that report once and run the script's cleanup, set with Set-Traps
.PP
Requires bash 5.3 or later, what current Linux ships. Under an older bash it exits 78, EX_CONFIG, before anything
else runs. macOS's /bin/bash is 3.2: install a current bash and put it ahead of /bin on PATH.
.PP
Requires GNU getopt, the one whose
.B getopt --test
exits 4. Where the getopt first on PATH is not GNU's, as macOS's own is not, it exits 78, EX_CONFIG, before anything
else runs: install MacPorts' util-linux, or Homebrew's gnu-getopt and put its bin directory first on PATH.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.B <script_path>
Path to the script being executed (usually $0).
.TP
.B <long-options>
Comma-separated list of long options (e.g., "help,verbose,path:"). Options requiring arguments should end with a colon.
.TP
.B <short-options>
String of short options (e.g., "hvp"). Options requiring arguments should be followed by a colon.
.TP
.B [arguments]
Arguments to pass to the script.
.SH PROVIDED FUNCTIONS
.TP
.B Set-Environment [--whitelist "VAR1 VAR2"] <envfile...>
Loads environment variables from one or more .env files. Supports optional whitelist to limit which variables are
exported.
.TP
.B Set-Traps [cleanup]
Sends ERR, HUP, INT and TERM to on_error_or_interrupt, and EXIT to the named cleanup function. See TRAPS.
.TP
.B on_error_or_interrupt <event>
The handler Set-Traps installs. A script does not call it itself.
.TP
.B usage <synopsis>
Displays the man page for the script if available, otherwise falls back to the provided synopsis.
.TP
.B error <exit_code> <message>
Prints an error message to stderr with a red ✘ symbol. Exits with the given code if non-zero.
.TP
.B note <message>
Prints an informational message to stderr with a + symbol. Respects SILENT environment variable.
.TP
.B success <message>
Prints a success message to stderr with a green ✔ symbol.
.TP
.B require_command <name...>
Stops the script with exit 69, EX_UNAVAILABLE, when a tool it names is not found, naming every one that is missing.
Each name is looked up with
.BR hash ,
which searches PATH and accepts a function of that name.
.SH PROVIDED VARIABLES
.TP
.B script_root
The directory containing the script.
.TP
.B script_name
The basename of the script.
.TP
.B script_arguments
Parsed arguments from getopt.
.TP
.B b, n, u, u_
Terminal formatting codes for bold, normal, underline, and underline-off.
.SH USAGE
.RS
.nf
[[ -r "$(dirname "$0")/Declare-BashScript" ]] || {
    printf '%s: cannot find Declare-BashScript in %s\\n' "${0##*/}" "$(dirname "$0")" >&2
    exit 72 # EX_OSFILE
}

# shellcheck source=Declare-BashScript
source "$(dirname "$0")/Declare-BashScript" "$0" "help,verbose,path:" "hvp" "$@"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check for the file before sourcing it. This script defines the error functions and turns on
errexit, so a consumer cannot report its absence through it; and a failed
.B source
leaves the consumer's option loop calling an undefined
.BR error ,
which neither exits nor shifts, so the loop never ends. Exit 72, EX_OSFILE, is the sysexits code for a
critical file that is missing. Do not write
.B source ... || exit
instead: the helper would then run with errexit suppressed.
.SH TRAPS
.RS
.nf
function cleanup {
    [[ -z "${download_dir}" ]] || rm -rf "${download_dir}"
}

Set-Traps cleanup
.fi
.RE
.PP
A script that traps calls
.B Set-Traps
once, after the cleanup function it names is defined and before its arguments are parsed, and sets no trap of its
own. A script with nothing to undo calls it with no argument.
.PP
The helper turns on errtrace, so a failure inside a function reaches the handler, and inherit_errexit, so a failure
inside $(...) fails the command that holds it. A failure that ends the script is reported once, as
.B error
reports, with its line and command, and the script exits with the failing command's status. For a pipeline, the
command named is the pipeline's last. A failure the script tolerates, under
.BR "set +o errexit" ,
in a test such as
.BR if ,
or before
.BR || ,
is not reported. A failure inside a subshell or a command substitution is reported by the shell that started it.
.PP
SIGHUP, SIGINT and SIGTERM are reported and end the script with 128 plus the signal's number: 129, 130 and 143.
.PP
The cleanup runs on every way out: a failure, a signal, an
.B error
call and a normal end. It reads the exit status from
.BR $? ,
so a script that undoes only on failure says so in its cleanup.
